            --rl-rule as a ground and lets it show through a 1px gap, which
            needs every cell to be opaque in the panel's own colour -- a colour
            that is --rl-ink in light and --rl-surface-raised in dark, so each
            cell would need the same `dark:` variant and the same two global.css
            rules as the panel itself, for a line nobody would ever see move.
            Worse, its rule colour is wrong here: #d4d4d8 against near-black is
            a bright lattice, where the same token against white is the hairline
            it was chosen to be.

            So the boxes draw their own borders in `currentColor` at 20%, which
            resolves against whichever ground the panel is carrying. EVERY CELL
            DRAWS ITS TOP AND LEFT ONLY and the nav closes the frame with its
            own right and bottom, so each line in the matrix is exactly one
            border thick. The first version lapped full borders together with
            `-mr-px -mb-px` instead, which stacks two translucent borders on
            every inner edge: about 36% against 20% on the outer frame,
            invisible with an opaque rule and obvious with alpha, and worst at
            two columns where the inner edges outnumber the outer.

            `data-ops-index` is a TEST HOOK, the same declared convention as the
            two below it: tests/ops-page.test.ts reads every href out of this
            nav and fails on one that names an anchor the page does not render.
          */}
